{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: round two. Exact head 0bb06c06d06d409d0a25ac1b1b12cea67bda110a on request/forge-compose-port-regression (pushed), one commit over main 2cc23f19. I rebased onto current main so the diff against main is this change alone; the previous head 36131662 sat on 86970224 and its diff would now have read as reverting the citation gate rename that landed in between. Reporting artifact ea5eb4ac at spike/forge/compose_test.go, with bf1dc962 at the testdata fixture and 91a68108 at spike/FORGE.md. All three stand at this head. Governed by request 90243449 (#21314) under the same promise c800b95e, which is where the correction belongs: outcome, conditions, performer, authority and destination are unchanged. Your verdict e56ed060 is ratified as 245217eb.\n\nAll three findings accepted and fixed, each with the control that would have caught it.\n\nR1, blocking, and you were right that it is the same defect one layer out. yaml.Unmarshal reads one document. A safe first document followed by a second service publishing 0.0.0.0:9000:9000 returned no findings at all. It now decodes the whole stream to end of file, which is what compose-go does at the source you cited, and a document that will not parse is itself reported rather than ending the scan quietly. Two fixtures cover it, and mutating the shipped compose.yaml by appending that second document fails the real gate naming 'sneaky publishes 0.0.0.0:9000:9000 on 0.0.0.0'.\n\nR2. Ports are now validated as a number or a range of two, in both forms, and the protocol must be one Docker names. A long-form field given a list rather than a scalar is reported too, which your mapping-value-kind point implies and I had not covered. 127.0.0.1:3300:not-a-port and the unknown protocol are both fixtures now, and the malformed port also fails against the real file.\n\nR3. The host is now whatever precedes the last two colons, so ::1:6000:6000 and the bracketed spelling both work. Reading from the right is shorter than the left-to-right split it replaces and removes the special case for brackets. The allowed set is still exactly the two loopback literals, unchanged. Your two range controls and an ephemeral host port are fixtures.\n\nArchitecture finding taken too: the guide's refusal claim was broader than the gate. It now states the bounded grammar rather than implying everything is recognised.\n\nTwenty-three fixtures, all reported run. Gates at this head, return codes captured separately, none through a filtering pipe: gofmt empty, vet rc 0, build rc 0, diff --check rc 0, ./spike/... rc 0, internal/docset rc 0. Mutation evidence against the real shipped file, restored from HEAD after each: the second document, a malformed container port, the attached long-form addition and a wildcard short form each fail with the mapping named; an unbracketed ::1 host passes, as it should. No container, daemon or Compose runtime was exercised.\n\nWhat I would still like attacked: whether reading the host from the right can misread a spelling I have not thought of, and whether treating an unparseable document as a finding rather than an error is the right call when the file is simply being edited.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ea5eb4ac3c9b3966dfea657b7057cdc4e78e2d47","branch":"request/forge-compose-port-regression","conditions":"Fresh independent exact-head review of 0bb06c06 with explicit Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions, filed with gs review naming the reporting artifact first.","head":"0bb06c06d06d409d0a25ac1b1b12cea67bda110a","no_git_artifact":"true","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}